Keep recording through a mute while the echo canceller is transparent

Patch 0010 stops the audio device's recording when the last stream sending sound goes (a
microphone turned off in a call), and at the unmute starts it again and restarts AEC3 for
recording, keeping its transparent mode (with a headset: that there is no echo). While
recording is stopped AEC3 hears nothing of the room, so nothing it keeps can be checked
until the unmute: a headset taken off for speakers with no change of devices anyone could
tell keeps the headset's transparent mode and lets the speakers' echo through after the
unmute (0010: -15 dBFS, heard 1.1 s), and each stop and start of the device is one more
chance for the sound server or the driver to move things (patch 0011: a Bluetooth headset
switching profile as recording stops and starts, its sink and source removed and added).

Now, while AEC3 is in its transparent mode, the microphone stays open through the mute.
When the last sending stream goes in a call that goes on playing (the same test as 0010's
stopped_in_call_), and the audio processing says its echo controller was transparent after
the last recorded frame, AudioState does not stop recording and marks it held. The audio
transport already processes what is recorded with no stream, in the last streams' format
(patch 0010), and sends it nowhere (AudioTransportImpl; a transport given to the factory,
patch 0002, has to do the same); AEC3 goes on hearing the room. The hold ends, and
recording stops as before (stopped_in_call_ stays true, so the unmute is 0010's), as soon
as the echo controller is no longer transparent (an echo path found: speakers; or the
controller made anew). It ends too, as stopped_in_call_ does, when a stream received comes
or goes, playout or recording is turned off, or the call ends; and at a change of audio
devices (AudioDevicesChangedCount moved), after which the unmute takes the devices as
changed (patch 0016's restart for other devices, forgetting the transparent mode) even
when a device's own restart while held handled the change (patch 0014's realign keeps the
transparent mode, which a headset taken off must not). At an unmute with recording held
nothing is started: the echo controller is restarted for recording as at any unmute
(RestartEchoControllerForRecording, or RestartEchoControllerForOtherDevices after a change),
and the hold is cleared. A device that stopped recording by itself while held is started
as usual.

How AEC3 tells it: EchoControl::TransparentModeActive (false unless overridden), which
EchoCanceller3 answers from its block processor, its echo remover and AecState's transparent
mode; AudioProcessingImpl stores it in an atomic after each ProcessStream, with its capture
lock held, processed or not (false with no echo controller), and
AudioProcessing::EchoControllerTransparent reads it from any thread. AEC3 keeps updating
its transparent mode while the processing's output is unused (patch 0008: nothing sends).

Threads: AudioState reads the atomic on the worker thread, where it makes every other call
to the audio device module, at the mute and then every 10 ms while recording is held (a
repeating task, stopped when the hold ends), and stops recording there. Nothing is added to
the capture path but the atomic's store: a hook in AudioTransportImpl would miss the
transport the wrapper gives the factory (patch 0002), and would have to post to the worker
anyway, with a safety flag for AudioState going away. Recording stops up to 10 ms (and
the worker's own delay) after AEC3 leaves its transparent mode; what is recorded meanwhile
is processed and dropped as any frame with no stream.

The cost: with a headset the microphone stays open during the mute while AEC3 is in
transparent mode: the system shows it in use, the device records and the audio processing
runs for nothing sent, until the hold ends; with speakers (no transparent mode) recording
stops at the mute as before. A held mute that AEC3 never leaves (a headset all along) keeps
the microphone open until the unmute or the call's end. If something processing what is
recorded stopped calling ProcessStream while recording is held, the last value read stays,
and so does the hold, until one of the other ends above.

Measured: not yet.

